  "summary_en": "Critical review of six EU Horizon 2020 social robotics projects for autism interventions, using critical disability and autism studies. It finds the main ethical shortcoming is lack of reflection on applying neuronormative behavioural interventions to a neurominority, risking epistemic injustice and technoableism, and questions whether ethical assistive robots can exist.",
  "key_provisions_or_claims": [
   "Analyses six Horizon 2020 robot-assisted autism intervention projects (BabyRobot, CARER-AID, DE-ENIGMA, DREAM, PlusMe, IM-TWIN).",
   "Main shortcoming: no critical reflection on ethical acceptability of neuronormative interventions on autistic people.",
   "Robot-assisted interventions risk normalization, technoableism and epistemic injustice.",
   "Ethics concepts for socially assistive robotics are vague and fragmented.",
   "Uses counterventionist approach and autistic community perspectives."
